    "aspects": [
      {
        "id": "amortized",
        "says": "ArrayList's append is amortized O(1): a resize copies every element, but growing the capacity by half each time keeps the total copying O(n).",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember",
          "example:39-data-structures/README_10.1.2.md:11"
        ],
        "exercise": "growable-array",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "best-worst-case",
        "says": "The same algorithm can have different best and worst cases: insertion sort is O(n) on sorted input and O(n^2) on reversed input.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Best, Average, and Worst Case",
          "example:39-data-structures/README_10.1.2.md:15"
        ],
        "exercise": "insertion-sort",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "drop-constants",
        "says": "Big O is an upper bound on growth that drops constants and lower-order terms, so it predicts scaling, not which code is faster.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Concept Explanation",
          "example:39-data-structures/README_10.1.2.md:7"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a statement about how to read the notation; the practices grade growth by counting operations, and actual speed can only be measured by timing"
      },
      {
        "id": "hashmap-worst-case",
        "says": "HashMap is O(1) on average, but many colliding keys make it O(log n) when they are Comparable and O(n) when they are not.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember",
          "example:39-data-structures/README_10.1.2.md:5"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "java21-tools",
        "says": "Parallel streams, virtual threads and Stream.toList can change constants or wall-clock time, not an algorithm's complexity.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Relevant Java 21 Features"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "incidental detail, not practised"
      },
      {
        "id": "logarithmic",
        "says": "An O(log n) algorithm such as binary search halves the remaining range with each step, so a million elements take about 20 probes.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Time Complexity",
          "example:39-data-structures/README_10.1.2.md:9"
        ],
        "exercise": "binary-search",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "memoization",
        "says": "Memoization trades O(n) space for time, turning the O(2^n) recursive Fibonacci into O(n).",
        "basis": [
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them",
          "example:39-data-structures/README_10.1.2.md:2"
        ],
        "exercise": "memo-fibonacci",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "mid-overflow",
        "says": "The middle index is left + (right - left) / 2, because (left + right) / 2 overflows on large ranges.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling"
        ],
        "exercise": "binary-search",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "quicksort-worst-case",
        "says": "Quicksort degrades to O(n^2) when pivots are extreme, as a first-element pivot is on sorted input; a median-of-three pivot and a partition that splits equal elements avoid it.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "example:39-data-structures/README_10.1.2.md:15"
        ],
        "exercise": "quicksort-pivot",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "sort-stability",
        "says": "Java sorts objects with TimSort because it is stable, and primitives with dual-pivot quicksort, where stability has no meaning.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "example:39-data-structures/README_10.1.2.md:19"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
